Contributing to Leonix
======================

Leonix takes changes as pull requests on GitHub, at
https://github.com/w3ngzv/Leonix.  This file covers what a change needs
before it is sent.  A Chinese version is in CONTRIBUTING.zh_CN.


Getting the tree
----------------

The programs in /bin and their assembler are git submodules under
external/, and "make check" needs the assembler:

	git clone --recurse-submodules https://github.com/w3ngzv/Leonix.git

Documentation/building.rst lists the toolchain for each system.  The
QEMU test also needs qemu-system-avr.


Coding style
------------

The C and assembly code follows Linux's
Documentation/process/coding-style.rst, with the additions in
Documentation/coding-style.rst.  Most of the additions come from the
target: int is 16 bits under avr-gcc, interrupts and tasks share
2.5 KiB of SRAM, and the EEPROM can lose power in the middle of a
write.

Code comments are written in English or in Chinese; one language is
enough.  A comment explains what the code alone does not make clear.


Checks
------

Run these before sending.  All of them must pass:

	scripts/checkstyle.sh -g main..    # checkpatch, on each commit
	scripts/checkstyle.sh              # checkpatch, kernel-doc, width
	make                               # the image
	make check                         # host tests
	make check-qemu                    # lxfs test built by avr-gcc

A change to the filesystem's write paths needs a case in the reset test
of tests/test_lxfs.c, which cuts power at every byte of an operation.

A change that alters an image states the size output before and after
in the commit message, avr-size for the Leonardo and arm-none-eabi-size
for the STM32F103, for each architecture whose image changes.  A change
to arch/avr, arch/arm or drivers says whether it was run on a board,
and what was checked there.  Code shared by both architectures is built
for both, and the STM32 build is run with "make ARCH=arm check-boot".


Commits
-------

Each commit is one change, and the tree builds and passes the checks
at every commit, so that any one of them can be reverted on its own.

The subject names the area and the change, under 72 columns, with no
full stop:

	tests: 在 qemu-system-avr 中运行 lxfs 测试
	scripts: checkstyle.sh 不检查 include/leonix/kconfig.h

The body explains why the change is needed: the failure it fixes, the
datasheet section or the Linux commit it follows.  The diff already
shows what changed, so the body does not repeat it.  Chinese or
English are both accepted.

End the message with a Signed-off-by line in your real name:

	Signed-off-by: Your Name <you@example.org>

The line certifies the Developer Certificate of Origin 1.1, as in Linux
(Documentation/process/submitting-patches.rst, "Sign your work"): that
you wrote the change or have the right to submit it under the licence
of the files it touches.


Submodules
----------

Changes to the shell, the commands or the assembler go to their own
repositories, listed in external/README.  Moving a submodule to a newer
commit is a commit of its own in this tree, made after the kernel has
been built and tested against it.


References
----------

A datasheet or other document the code relies on is listed in
Documentation/references/index.rst with its document number and
revision, and cited in the comment by table or section.  Commit the
file itself only if its licence allows redistribution.


Licence
-------

Leonix is GPL-2.0-only.  A new file starts with an
SPDX-License-Identifier line, as the existing files do.
